## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of basic synchronization primitives and common OS patterns, especially producer-consumer, deadlock avoidance, and correct semaphore/mutex usage in C.

## Key Concepts
- Semaphores
- Mutex
- Circular buffer
- Deadlock detection
- Boundary conditions (full/empty)

## Code Example
```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>
#include <stdio.h>

#define N 10

int buffer[N];
int in = 0, out = 0;
sem_t empty, full;
pthread_mutex_t mutex;

void produce(int item) {
  sem_wait(&empty);
  pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
  buffer[in] = item;
  in = (in + 1) % N;
  pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
  sem_post(&full);
}

int consume() {
  int item;
  sem_wait(&full);
  pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
  item = buffer[out];
  out = (out + 1) % N;
  pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
  sem_post(&empty);
  return item;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to multiple producers/consumers and ensure fairness?
- What are trade-offs vs. using condition variables instead of semaphores?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical synchronization primitives in a kernel-like setting, not just theory.

## Key Concepts
- Wait queues, locks, memory ordering
- Spurious wakeups and rechecking condition
- Correct wakeup under contention

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct waiter { struct waiter *next; task_t *t; } waiter_t;

typedef struct wq { spinlock_t lock; waiter_t *head; bool *flag; } wq_t;

void wait_on(wq_t *q, bool *flag){
  acquire(&q->lock);
  while (!*flag){
    enqueue(q, current); // add to wait list
    release(&q->lock);
    schedule();
    acquire(&q->lock);
  }
  release(&q->lock);
}

void wake_up(wq_t *q){
  acquire(&q->lock);
  *q->flag = true;
  wake_all(q); // wake all waiters
  release(&q->lock);
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to handle timeouts?
- How to prevent missed wakeups when wake happens before wait is enqueued?
- Compare to condition variables vs. spin-wait approaches.

