## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of memory ordering, lock-free data structures, and weak memory models in OS CPUs. It probes ability to identify a real race and reason about inter-thread visibility across architectures.

## Key Concepts

- Lock-free synchronization
- memory_order_release
- memory_order_acquire
- Data visibility before signaling availability

## Code Example

```cpp
// Pseudo-C++ lock-free sketch
std::atomic<size_t> head{0}, tail{0};
int data[N];

void produce(int v){
  auto t = tail.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
  data[t % N] = v;
  tail.store((t+1) % N, std::memory_order_release);
}

int consume(){
  auto t = tail.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
  auto h = head.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
  if (h == t) return -1;
  int v = data[h % N];
  head.store((h+1) % N, std::memory_order_release);
  return v;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this pattern for multiple producers/consumers?
- What tests would you add to validate correctness across ARM/x86?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests understanding of inter-process sharing via mmap, page cache semantics, and memory visibility without user-space locking.

## Key Concepts
- MAP_SHARED page mappings
- Page cache dirty bits and writeback
- Cache coherence and TLB effects
- Lightweight synchronization in shared memory (CAS, acquire/release)

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudo: writer uses CAS to acquire lock, writes, then releases
uint32_t *lock = (uint32_t*)addr; // shared region
while (__sync_lock_test_and_set(lock, 1)) {}
// write 8 bytes to region after lock
memcpy((void*)(addr+8), data, 8);
__sync_lock_release(lock);

// Reader checks lock before reading
while (*lock) {} // wait for writer
memcpy(data, (void*)(addr+8), 8);
```

## Tradeoffs
Lock: Simple, ensures exclusive access but blocks readers during writes.
Seqlock: Readers can proceed concurrently but must retry if write occurs mid-read; better for read-heavy workloads.

